I believe only Gemini 4 Carbon beats this with a score of like 54 or something.

95
00:09:56,910 --> 00:09:59,280
但让我印象深刻的是实际应用结果。
But the real world results are what stood out to me.

96
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:09,700
OpenAI 表示，在对早期 Frontier 模型进行广泛测试后，
它在 Firefox 中发现了 81 个此前未知的严重漏洞。
OpenAI says it found 81 previously unknown high severity vulnerabilities in Firefox,
even after extensive testing with earlier Frontier models.

97
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:19,520
而在 Linux 中，他们的流程产生了 948 个可用的本地权限提升漏洞，
这意味着已经拥有受限访问权限的人可能会利用这些漏洞获得完整的管理员权限。
And in Linux, their pipeline produced 948 working local privilege escalation exploits,
meaning someone who already has limited access to a machine could

98
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:23,840
而在 Linux 中，他们的流程产生了 948 个可用的本地权限提升漏洞，
这意味着已经拥有受限访问权限的人可能会利用这些漏洞获得完整的管理员权限。
potentially use one of these flaws to gain full administrator access.

99
00:10:24,110 --> 00:10:26,480
他们还生成了待审核的修复方案。
They also generated candidate fixes for review.

100
00:10:26,702 --> 00:10:31,012
这次运行大约花费了 120 万美元的 API 使用费用。
The run cost about $1.2 million in API usage.

101
00:10:31,246 --> 00:10:32,996
所以这是一项严肃的研究工作。
So this was a serious research effort.

102
00:10:33,294 --> 00:10:38,148
当然，你还需要确保这个东西严格遵守它被允许测试的系统范围。
Of course, you also need this thing to stick to the systems it's actually allowed to test.

103
00:10:38,478 --> 00:10:40,324
你肯定不想再发生类似 hugging face 的事件。
You don't want another hugging face incident.

104
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:51,652
在一次评估中，他们故意在授权范围之外设置了简单的目标，而在其部署安全指令的约束下，
它在所有测试案例中都保持在范围内，这是一个好迹象。
In one evaluation, they deliberately put easy targets outside its authorized scope, and with its deployment safety instructions,
it stayed within bounds in every tested case, which is a good sign.

105
00:10:51,854 --> 00:10:58,500
不出所料，最初的访问权限仅限于通过 Codex 和 API 获取的获批 daybreak red 客户。
And unsurprisingly, access is initially limited to approved daybreak red customers through Codex and the API.

106
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:01,360
但是没错，这里的竞争态势非常明显。
But yeah, the race here is pretty clear.

107
00:11:01,646 --> 00:11:08,160
在这些模型落入试图破解它们的人手中之前，我们能利用这些模型保护多少软件？
How much software can we secure using these models before these models end up in the hands of people trying to break into it?

108
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:09,668
这就是目前的全部博弈。
That's the whole game right now.

109
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:22,400
现在，这些不断增长的能力也开始出现在科学研究中，这带我们进入另一个有趣的更新，
来自 Anthropic 的一篇名为「利用 Claude 研究 Vault 颗粒」的文章。
Now, these growing capabilities are also starting to show up in scientific research,
which brings us to another interesting update from Anthropic titled Investigating Vault Particles with Claude.

110
00:11:22,606 --> 00:11:25,956
他们显然一直在研究这些被称为 vault 颗粒的东西。
They've apparently been studying these things called vault particles.

111
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:33,348
基本上，它们是发现于我们细胞中的微小中空蛋白质
壳，科学家们至今仍未完全搞清楚它们的具体用途。
Basically, tiny hollow protein shells found in our cells, whose exact purpose scientists still haven't really figured out yet.

112
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:45,280
在 Claude 的帮助下，团队绘制出了一个感染细菌的病毒中相关 vault 的结构，
其思路是研究这些简单的版本可以帮助我们理解人类的 vault 到底起什么作用。
And with Claude's help, the team mapped the structure of a related vault from a virus that infects bacteria,
with the idea being that studying these simpler versions could help us understand what the human ones actually do.

113
00:11:45,550 --> 00:11:52,740
Claude 还帮助改进了他们的实验室流程，将准备显微镜样本的时间从几天缩短到约 90 分钟。
Claude also helped improve their lab process, cutting the time to prepare samples for microscopy from days to about 90 minutes.

114
00:11:53,166 --> 00:12:02,404
然后它还帮助处理显微镜数据，仅用 34 小时就完成了第一个
完整的结构模型，据他们估计，正常情况下这需要几周时间。
Then it helped process the microscope data, getting a first full structure model ready in just 34 hours,
which they estimate would normally have taken them weeks.

115
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:07,588
所以这是 AI 加速团队工作流的又一个例子。
So this is just another example of AI speeding up a team's workflow.

116
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:11,204
在这种情况下，是帮助科学家在实验室中测试想法。
In this case, helping scientists test ideas in the lab.

117
00:12:11,470 --> 00:12:24,548
然后在数学方面，Axiom 发布了 Stacks 项目的正式化版本，如果你没听说过，
这基本上是一部庞大的、大约 7500 页的代数几何参考著作。
And then on the math side, Axiom released a formalization of the Stacks project, which, if you've never heard of it,
is basically a massive, roughly 7,500 page reference work in algebraic geometry.

118
00:12:24,782 --> 00:12:30,960
他们将其翻译成 lean，这样计算机就可以检查证明，而这些结果随后可以在其他证明中重复使用。
They have translated it into lean, where a computer can check the proofs, and those results can then be reused in other proofs.

119
00:12:31,342 --> 00:12:40,352
你可以把它想象成把这庞大的数学体系变成一个人们和
AI 系统都可以构建的库，而且逻辑步骤会自动检查。
Think of it as turning this huge body of mathematics into a library that people and AI systems can build on,
with the logical steps checked automatically.

120
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:50,404
仍然需要人工审核，以确保 AI 编写的正式化内容与原始数学内容完全匹配，
但他们现在已经拥有了一个相当重要的基础。
There's still human review needed to make sure the AI written formalizations faithfully match the original mathematics,
but that's a pretty substantial foundation they can now build on.

121
00:12:50,912 --> 00:12:51,792
所以没错。
So yeah.

122
00:12:52,046 --> 00:13:03,232
我们看到了 AI 帮助构建更好的 AI，AI 在科学和数学领域取得进展，
而且 AI 在发现现实世界软件中的安全漏洞方面能力越来越强。
We've got AI helping build better AI, AI making progress in science and math, and
AI getting increasingly capable at finding security flaws in real-world software.

123
00:13:03,438 --> 00:13:07,428
这些基本上就是 AI 目前正在全力冲刺的主要领域。
These are pretty much the main areas AI is going ham in right now.

124
00:13:07,712 --> 00:13:22,672
但这带到了本周的另一个大新闻，我敢肯定你们都在密切关注，尤其是我的美国观众，
因为公众对这一切的反应正开始变成一个真正的问题，至少对于那些 AI 爱好者来说是这样。
But this brings us to the other big story this week, which I'm sure you've all been following closely, especially my US audience,
because the public response to all this is starting to become a real problem, at least for the AI enthusiasts out there.

125
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:31,312
